<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mentuhotep II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 2055–2004 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/21st-century-bce">21st century BCE</a> · Also known as Nebhepetre Mentuhotep</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mentuhotep II was a king of the Theban Eleventh Dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. Around 2055 BCE he defeated the rival Herakleopolitan kings and reunited the country after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-intermediate-period">First Intermediate Period</a>, an achievement usually taken as the start of the Middle Kingdom. His terraced mortuary temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deir-el-bahari">Deir el-Bahari</a>, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a>, later inspired the temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hatshepsut">Hatshepsut</a> beside it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mentuhotep-ii.jpg" alt="Mentuhotep II" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Richard Mortel · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Statue_of_Montuhotep_from_Deir_al-Bahri,_11th_Dynasty,_2065-2014_BCE;_Egyptian_Museum,_Cairo_(3).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Mentuhotep II succeeded his father Intef III on the throne at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a> and ruled for 51 years according to the Turin Canon. He inherited the lands his predecessors had conquered from the first cataract in the south to Abydos in the north. The rival 10th Dynasty ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-egypt">Lower Egypt</a> from Herakleopolis. His first fourteen years seem peaceful. In his fourteenth year, named the Year of the crime of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thinis">Thinis</a>, the Herakleopolitan kings apparently desecrated the ancient royal necropolis at Abydos, and he sent his armies north. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deir-el-bahari">Deir el-Bahari</a> a tomb held the linen-wrapped bodies of 60 soldiers killed in battle, their shrouds bearing his cartouche, and it is believed to be that of men who died in this conflict.</p><p>Reunification probably came shortly before his 39th regnal year, when he changed his titulary to Sematawy, 'He who unifies the two lands'. The process took time: commoners were buried with weapons, and an expedition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/land-of-punt">Punt</a> sent by his successor some twenty years later still had to clear the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wadi-hammamat">Wadi Hammamat</a> of rebels loyal to the fallen Herakleopolitan rulers. In his 29th and 31st years his vizier Khety led campaigns into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a>, the first attested appearance of the term Kush in Egyptian records, and a garrison was posted at Elephantine.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>After unification he reversed the decentralisation that had marked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-intermediate-period">First Intermediate Period</a>. Nomarchs, whose offices had become hereditary in the 6th Dynasty, lost some of their power. He created the posts of Governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-egypt">Upper Egypt</a> and Governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-egypt">Lower Egypt</a> over them and sent officials from the capital to travel the country and supervise regional leaders. Nomarchs who had supported the 10th Dynasty, such as the governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asyut">Asyut</a>, certainly lost their power. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">viziers</a> were Bebi and Dagi, and the treasurer Meketre is among his officials.</p><p>His titularies show a return to Old Kingdom tradition and a programme of self-deification. His subjects regarded him as divine or half divine, and stelae of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senusret-iii">Senusret III</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amenemhat-iii">Amenemhat III</a>, some 200 years later, record ceremonies performed on his statues.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Mentuhotep II was buried at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deir-el-bahari">Deir el-Bahari</a>. His terraced mortuary temple, Akh-sut Nebhepetre, broke with the Old Kingdom pyramid complex and was the earliest mortuary temple where the king himself enacts ceremonies for the gods and is identified with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osiris">Osiris</a>. It inspired the temples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hatshepsut">Hatshepsut</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thutmose-iii">Thutmose III</a>, built about 550 years later. Lord Dufferin began excavating it in 1859, Howard Carter found the black seated Ka statue of the king in 1898, and Naville, Winlock and Dieter Arnold followed.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mentuhotep II” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Mentuhotep II</b> (Nebhepetre), who came to the throne at Thebes around 2055 BCE, fought the Herakleopolitans and their allies in Middle Egypt and, probably within the first four decades of a reign of about fifty years, reunited the country; he marked the achievement by taking the Horus name…</blockquote><a href="/empire/middle-kingdom-of-egypt">Middle Kingdom of Egypt · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 2025 BCE <b>Mentuhotep II</b> of Thebes took Herakleopolis and reunited Egypt, opening the Middle Kingdom of Egypt.</blockquote><a href="/state/herakleopolitan-kingdom">Herakleopolitan Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Mentuhotep II</b>, who came to the throne around 2055 BCE, a date often used for the start of the Middle Kingdom of Egypt, later defeated Herakleopolis and reunited the country.</blockquote><a href="/state/theban-eleventh-dynasty">Theban Eleventh Dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
